Hey!

First of all: congrats on the release of angular/aria! I've recently started experimenting with the aria directives
to see if they could integrate into our corporate libs.

While doing that, I noticed that many class members are public, and I'm trying to understand the intended encapsulation model. For example: does `_` mean private in some way?

<img width="815" height="650" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/98689641-2670-4812-b655-dcb0f6c2ab5a" />


I'm probably missing some context, but I'm aware of the following points:

1. You've recently exposed directive tokens as part of the public API, in order to create components by subclassing directives (customizations).

2. The general tension around the class itself: a single class provides

   - the interface a directive is initialized with (inputs/outputs),
   - what it exposes at the template / query / injection level,
   - internal communication between directives via tokens (provide / useExisting),
   - what can be extended via subclassing.

3. TypeScript private fields in classes are accessible anyway: `myClass['privateField']`. And some APIs let you retrieve private providers defined in children: `viewChild(ChildComp, {read: PrivateChildProvider})`.

4. The tests seem purely behaviour-driven, with no usage of `_` methods.

I guess this touches on quite a few things. I was mostly wondering whether keeping the public surface of the directives smaller — e.g. via `private` / `protected` / `#` / `@internal` — is something you've considered. Or is the current broad surface intentional maybe driven by the points above and material conventions?

Thanks a lot!

